WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:01.945
Your pipeline is slow.

2
00:00:01.965 --> 00:00:04.605
But the cause is inside the database.

3
00:00:04.625 --> 00:00:06.330
Why does change capture lag?

4
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.840
Why does a replica return old rows?

5
00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:14.840
Three ideas answer most of them: the storage engine, transactions, and replication.

6
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.475
First: how does a database store rows?

7
00:00:17.495 --> 00:00:19.837
A B-tree keeps data sorted in pages.

8
00:00:19.857 --> 00:00:21.050
Reads are fast.

9
00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.635
Each write updates a page in place.

10
00:00:23.655 --> 00:00:25.423
An LSM-tree only appends.

11
00:00:25.443 --> 00:00:26.948
Writes are very fast.

12
00:00:26.968 --> 00:00:29.895
Later, it merges files in the background.

13
00:00:29.915 --> 00:00:31.807
So: many reads, pick a B-tree.

14
00:00:31.827 --> 00:00:34.920
Very heavy writes, pick an LSM-tree.

15
00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:37.655
Second: how does a database stay correct?

16
00:00:37.675 --> 00:00:41.965
Before it changes any data, the database writes the change to a log.

17
00:00:41.985 --> 00:00:44.165
After a crash, it replays the log.

18
00:00:44.185 --> 00:00:46.575
Nothing that was committed is lost.

19
00:00:46.595 --> 00:00:49.363
Change data capture reads that same log.

20
00:00:49.383 --> 00:00:52.925
That is why CDC does not load the tables.

21
00:00:52.945 --> 00:00:55.860
Third: copies of the data, on other machines.

22
00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:57.398
A leader takes the writes.

23
00:00:57.418 --> 00:00:59.920
Replicas copy them, a little later.

24
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.283
So a read from a replica can be stale.

25
00:01:02.303 --> 00:01:04.705
This is called replication lag.

26
00:01:04.725 --> 00:01:10.280
And when the network splits, you must choose: stay consistent, or stay available.

27
00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:13.115
Last: read from a source without hurting it.

28
00:01:13.135 --> 00:01:15.115
A full dump is simple but heavy.

29
00:01:15.135 --> 00:01:17.503
An updated-at filter misses deletes.

30
00:01:17.523 --> 00:01:20.840
Log-based CDC sees everything.

31
00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:23.600
Quick recap of database foundations.

32
00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.075
B-tree for reads, LSM for writes.

33
00:01:26.095 --> 00:01:27.400
The log comes first.

34
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.210
And replicas lag.

35
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.330
Now start the first lesson below.
